---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const layers = [
  {
    title: 'Pi agent runtime',
    text: 'Pi provides the runtime surface: package loading, shell interaction, user workflow, and the place where cyb3r can operate. Think of it as the operating-system layer of the stack.',
  },
  {
    title: 'cyb3r agent harness',
    text: 'cyb3r is the operator harness built on top of that runtime. It coordinates reasoning, plans, tool use, evidence handling, and the loop between actions and reassessment.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reasoning model',
    text: 'The fine-tuned model provides dedicated security reasoning, branch ranking, pivot decisions, rabbit-hole avoidance, and controlled tool-call behavior. Think of it as the CPU of the harness.',
  },
  {
    title: 'pi-pen-kit tool layer',
    text: 'pi-pen-kit exposes controlled security-validation tooling to the harness, with listener and session management kept behind explicit operator workflows.',
  },
];

const toolExamples = [
  'Start and manage controlled reverse-shell listeners from AI workflows',
  'Catch interactive reverse shells and keep them usable across agent tool calls',
  'Read, write, interrupt, probe, and execute commands inside shell sessions',
  'Use Sliver C2 sessions, beacons, listeners, and implants through Pi tools',
  'Use Metasploit RPC for modules, payloads, jobs, sessions, and exploitation workflows',
];

const modelGoals = [
  'Structured security reasoning instead of generic assistant behavior',
  'Global reassessment during multi-step security validation tasks',
  'Ranking attack branches instead of brute-forcing the first idea',
  'Knowing when to abort low-yield rabbit holes',
  'Preserving tool-call competence while improving operator judgment',
];
---

<Layout
  title="cyb3r | Controlled security automation"
  description="How cyb3r is built: a Pi-based agent harness, a security reasoning model, controlled tooling, evidence handling and operator-supervised workflows."
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden pt-32 pb-20">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 hexagon-pattern opacity-20"></div>
    <div class="container-custom relative">
      <a href="/#engineering-depth" class="text-cyan hover:text-white transition-colors text-sm font-semibold">Back to engineering projects</a>

      <div class="mt-10 grid lg:grid-cols-[0.9fr_1.1fr] gap-10 lg:gap-16 items-center">
        <div class="reveal">
          <div class="mb-6 flex items-center gap-4">
            <div class="h-20 w-20 rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/70 p-3 flex items-center justify-center">
              <img src="/projects/cyb3r-logo.png" alt="cyb3r logo" class="h-full w-full object-contain" />
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-cyan font-mono text-sm">cyb3r</p>
              <p class="text-steel text-sm">controlled security automation</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-6xl font-bold leading-tight mb-6">
            <span class="text-white">An agent harness for</span>
            <span class="text-gradient"> controlled security workflows.</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="text-xl text-steel leading-relaxed">
            cyb3r is a research system built around Pi: an agent runtime, an operator harness,
            a dedicated reasoning model, and controlled tooling for repeatable security validation
            without losing operator oversight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-deep-space/70 p-5 reveal" style="transition-delay: 120ms">
          <img src="/projects/cyb3r-system.svg" alt="cyb3r architecture overview" class="w-full cursor-zoom-in rounded-md transition-opacity hover:opacity-90" data-expandable-diagram />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding relative">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mb-12 reveal">
        <p class="text-cyan font-mono text-sm mb-4">system model</p>
        <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-5">The stack has four layers</h2>
        <p class="text-xl text-steel leading-relaxed">
          cyb3r is designed less as “a model” and more as an operator system.
          The model matters, but it only becomes useful when it can drive tools,
          preserve context, and reason like an operator.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-5">
        {layers.map((layer, index) => (
          <article class="card-glass p-6 reveal" style={`transition-delay: ${index * 70}ms`}>
            <div class="glow-effect"></div>
            <div class="relative z-10">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">{layer.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-steel leading-relaxed text-sm">{layer.text}</p>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 hexagon-pattern opacity-15"></div>
    <div class="container-custom relative">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1fr] gap-10 lg:gap-14 items-start">
        <div class="reveal">
          <p class="text-cyan font-mono text-sm mb-4">pi-pen-kit</p>
          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">Security tools as controlled agent capabilities</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-steel leading-relaxed mb-6">
            pi-pen-kit is the bridge between cyb3r and the tools an operator actually uses.
            It is not just a list of command wrappers. It keeps interactive state alive so the
            agent can continue working across tool calls.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            {toolExamples.map(item => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 text-steel">
                <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 flex-shrink-0 rounded-full bg-cyan"></span>
                <span>{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-deep-space/70 p-8 reveal" style="transition-delay: 120ms">
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-5">Controlled reverse-shell sessions</h3>
          <p class="text-steel leading-relaxed mb-5">
            A normal `nc -lvnp 4444` listener is awkward for an agent because the session belongs
            to the shell process that started it. pi-netcat replaces that with a controlled shim and
            daemon: the listener owns sockets, the agent can list sessions, read buffered output,
            write commands, interrupt stuck processes, probe responsiveness, and close sessions.
          </p>
          <p class="text-steel leading-relaxed">
            That turns a fragile interactive reverse shell into a tool-controlled workflow that the
            harness can reason about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding relative">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.95fr_1.05fr] gap-10 lg:gap-14 items-center">
        <div class="reveal">
          <p class="text-cyan font-mono text-sm mb-4">model</p>
          <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-5">Training for operator reasoning</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-steel leading-relaxed mb-6">
            The current training target is a Qwen-class 27B model with a reasoning-first
            cybersecurity fine-tune. The first training pass spans epochs 01-03 and is running now.
            The initial focus is behavior: planning, reassessment, branch ranking, and tool-use
            discipline. Security knowledge expansion comes after the reasoning pass. In the stack,
            the model is best understood as the CPU that drives operator judgment inside the harness.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3">
            {modelGoals.map(goal => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 text-steel">
                <span class="mt-2 h-1.5 w-1.5 flex-shrink-0 rounded-full bg-red-400"></span>
                <span>{goal}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="reveal" style="transition-delay: 120ms">
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-deep-space/70 p-5 mb-5">
            <img src="/projects/cyb3r.png" alt="cyb3r model card teaser" class="w-full rounded-md" />
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-lg border border-red-400/30 bg-red-400/10 p-5">
            <h3 class="text-white font-semibold mb-2">Partners wanted</h3>
            <p class="text-steel text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Training hardware is currently the limiting factor. The project is open to
              partners who can help with compute, evaluation, applied security feedback, or
              controlled real-world workflows.
            </p>
            <a
              href="https://huggingface.co/Cyb3rDudu/cyb3r"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="mt-4 inline-flex text-sm font-semibold text-red-300 transition-colors hover:text-white"
            >
              Hugging Face model card
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-deep-space/70 p-5 reveal">
        <img src="/projects/cyb3r-training.svg" alt="cyb3r training direction overview" class="w-full cursor-zoom-in rounded-md transition-opacity hover:opacity-90" data-expandable-diagram />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
